          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 1,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "A popular primary school game is called ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Greedy Pig",
              "marks": [
                "italic"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ". In this game, a player rolls a six-sided die until they choose to stop. Their score is equal to the sum of rolled values. However, if they roll a 1 before they stop, their score is zero. At what score should a player choose to stop given that they want to maximise their expected score?",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Stop when the score is at least $20$.\n\nThe expected gain from another roll is $\\frac{(20-S)}{6}$, which is positive only when the current score $S<20$.",
      "worked": "Let $S$ be the current score and $G$ the gain from the next roll. Rolling a 1 loses $S$ points; rolling 2, 3, 4, 5 or 6 gains that many points. Each outcome has probability $\\frac{1}{6}$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}E(G)&=(-S)\\times\\frac16+2\\times\\frac16+3\\times\\frac16+4\\times\\frac16+5\\times\\frac16+6\\times\\frac16\\\\E(G)&=\\frac{20-S}{6}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe expected gain is positive for $S<20$ and negative for $S>20$. At $S=20$, another roll has zero expected gain. Continue while the score is below $20$, and stop when it reaches at least $20$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 55
  },
